WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:17.730
Lead is a very soft metal and it’s easily
moulded into all sorts

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:23.180
of shapes. This is a pot, I’m not sure what
it is from, I got it

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.800
from an old lab that was being closed down
so it was

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.500
probably made for holding a strong acid because
lead is very

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:36.410
unreactive. You can see rather nicely here
the white colour of

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:40.370
lead oxide because it has oxidised over the
years and this is

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.360
the white material that was used for paints.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.400
This white lead paint that people use, the
oxide of lead, had

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.540
one rather unfortunate consequence that it
reacts with

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:57.060
sulphur in the air and in Victorian times
when people burnt

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:01.840
coal they produced a lot of sulphur, and the
sulphur reacted

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.110
with the lead to give a black colour which
made the white

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.140
paint eventually turn grey and if you waited
long enough

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.610
black. And this effect was exploited by one
of my former

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.259
colleagues here Professor Leslie Crombie,
who during the war

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:23.789
was working for the British admiralty in their
research labs on

00:01:23.789 --> 00:01:29.310
the camouflaging of submarines. Now if you
want your

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.219
submarine not to be seen, when it is well
away from the

00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:36.039
coast it needs to be white because if you
have a white

00:01:36.039 --> 00:01:41.179
submarine it does not show up against the
sky but, if you are

00:01:41.179 --> 00:01:46.529
trying to slip into the coast at night and
not be seen you need

00:01:46.529 --> 00:01:50.920
the submarine to be black. So Leslie came
up with the idea of

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:56.039
painting the submarine with lead oxide and
when it was going

00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:00.189
near the coast, some sailors went out on to
the outside of the

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:06.719
ship and sloshed over a solution of sulphide
and seawater and

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:11.150
the paint turned black. And so you could then
go into the

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:14.300
coast, drop your agent, do whatever secret
things you

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.250
wanted to do, and then when you went away
from the coast

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.610
they sloshed hydrogen peroxide with seawater,
which turned

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:28.329
the sulphite into sulphate, and lead sulphate
is white so your

00:02:28.329 --> 00:02:31.409
submarine went white again. So you could change
the colour

00:02:31.409 --> 00:02:35.019
of your submarine to suit what you were doing
in the military

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:37.540
operation and nobody would see you.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.519
And that was a real thing that happened?

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:42.330
Yes, that was a real thing. I don’t know
how widely it was

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:46.040
used but they actually tried it out and it
worked. So they just

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.209
needed to take the chemicals and they could
add the

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:52.099
seawater to make the solution to put on the
submarine.

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:57.180
The other very important property of lead
is that because the

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:05.040
lead atom is heavy it is very good at stopping
radioactive

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.860
particles particularly alpha particles, which
are helium nuclei

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:15.689
those are the nucleus of the helium atom,
or beta particles

00:03:15.689 --> 00:03:16.810
which are electrons.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.269
What you should find that if we put this in
the way, it should

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:24.560
stop counting almost immediately so let us
see if that works.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.939
There we go straight back down to background
and if we take

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:33.219
it away, and it picks back up again.

00:03:33.219 --> 00:03:37.629
And so people who are working with radioactive
samples used

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:41.510
to use, and still use, bricks made out of
lead. So here are two

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:45.390
of the bricks and you can see this is a small
one and this one

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:50.189
is rather thinner. The problem is that if
you start building

00:03:50.189 --> 00:03:56.019
these bricks into a wall, there is always
a chance that when

00:03:56.019 --> 00:03:59.349
you have two bits together there might be
a slight gap

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:03.689
between the two bricks and some of the radioactive
particles

00:04:03.689 --> 00:04:08.489
may go between them. So to avoid this the
bricks are made

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:13.599
with groves in it, and a pointed part here,
so that when you

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:17.620
put them together there is absolutely no way
that anything

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.340
can go through because you can’t have a
direct line through

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.930
it. Now the trouble is of course you can’t
see anything

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:29.860
through it, so if you have an experiment where
you want to

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.080
handle radioactive material inside a container
and manipulate

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.530
it you can’t make the whole container of
lead because you

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:42.220
can’t see what is going on inside. And so
for that, people

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:48.850
have developed lead glass, of which this is
a sample and it is

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:54.180
immensely heavy, but this is glass and there
is really a large

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.110
amount of lead that has been put in it. And
some of the fancy

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:02.600
crystals that your grandmothers or your ancestors
might have

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.670
had, had a bit of lead in the glass. This
has got a really high

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.730
proportion of lead in the glass which is probably
why the

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:13.330
glass is yellow rather than the white colour
that you normally

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.380
have with glass. But windows like this can
be used for

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:20.930
handling really quite radioactive materials
behind them. The

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.190
colour is rather nice isn’t it?

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.210
Good.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:37.460
But it is very heavy, so…

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:43.630
Well, lead has had a very long history; it
was one of the first

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:48.260
metals to be widely used. The Romans used
this for drinking

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:52.690
materials. There is also lead acetate, that
is a compound of

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:57.710
lead and vinegar is meant to taste sweet and
the Romans

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:01.930
added this to their wine to make it sweeter.
The fact that it

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:07.090
was really very poisonous and caused, possibly
even caused

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:13.070
madness, was not really widely recognised.
In fact some

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.860
people have suggested that part of the collapse
of the Roman

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:20.570
Empire was due to too much lead. Lead was
very widely used

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:24.540
in the UK for water pipes because it’s easy
to manufacture

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.450
and to join together. It tends not to have
been very

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:32.110
dangerous in terms of drinking water because
it gets a

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:35.900
coating on the surface so you do not get much
lead in the

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:39.770
water; though if you do have a house with
lead pipes in, it is

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:47.390
usually advisable to change them. It’s again,
it was used very

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:53.990
widely from the 1920s up till the late 70s
or 80s as an

00:06:53.990 --> 00:07:00.270
additive for petrol. In the old days when
petrol engines were

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:06.100
first introduced, when you came to a hill
and put down the

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:11.370
accelerator of your car, the engine started
misfiring, so-called

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:14.480
‘pinking’, and the whole thing shook.
So it was really quite

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:18.940
difficult to drive up hills, but then it was
discovered that you

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:23.270
could put in a compound, so-called tetraethyl
lead, which was

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:29.970
really very poisonous, but the guy who discovered
it washed

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:34.071
his hands in public at a press conference
saying “look it’s so

00:07:34.071 --> 00:07:38.930
safe”, despite the fact that there were
people dying in his

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:43.540
factory. So it was very widely adopted and
so it was only in

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:47.120
quite recent times that it was realised how
much lead was

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:51.640
being blown out into the atmosphere in car
exhausts. And so

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:58.890
it’s now almost everywhere we have lead-free
petrol as a

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.949
result of people realising the poison-ness
of lead.

